# Half-Life snark. Measured by vrcardgen, finished by hand.
#
# Part J: "Take one from the nest on the chest. It squirms in the hand and
# bites if held too long (a small amount of damage, the Half-Life way).
# Throw it, or drop it at your feet and let it hunt."
#
# So it is the penguin's card, and for the same reason: a creature that is
# RELEASED rather than fired, with no fuse, because what happens after it
# leaves the hand is the snark's business and not the weapon's.
#
# THE MODEL IS TWO SKELETONS, AND ONLY ONE OF THEM IS THE WEAPON. `Bip01` is
# the player - two arms, two hands, ten fingers, 47 bones. `Bip02` hangs off
# `Bip01 R Hand` and is the snark: pelvis, two thighs and calves, spine,
# neck, two little arms, head, two mandibles (`Bone15`/`Bone16`,
# `Bone20`/`Bone21`), and a chain off the neck. There is no receiver, no
# barrel and no muzzle - the model declares no attachments at all, which on a
# viewmodel is the generator's muzzle, so there is nothing to aim.
#
# DELIBERATELY NO JOINTS, and this is the trap on this model. vrcardgen
# reports 12 drivable joints here and every one of them is wrong: they are
# the creature's anatomy, not a mechanism.
#
# The number this comment used to lead with was wrong twice over and is worth
# correcting precisely, because the conclusion is right and the reasoning for
# it should be too. It said `Bone16` was "the biggest mover in the model at
# 7.22 units ... a jaw opening in fidgetnip". Measured: 7.22 is in FIDGETFIT,
# not fidgetnip - fidgetnip moves Bone16 by 4.57. And Bone16's own local
# translation is 0.000 in all six sequences: it only rotates in place. The 7.22
# is its ORIGIN being carried about by the Bip02 Neck1/Head chain rotating
# above it, which is a different claim entirely and matters, because a joint
# measures the bone's own travel and this bone has none.
#
# The conclusion stands and is the reason for the whole comment: a joint
# declared here would hand that bone to the simulator, which poses it from a
# joint value, and the snark would hold its mouth open instead of nipping. The
# shock roach does declare its segments, because those move AS IT FIRES;
# nothing on the snark does, so the snark's own sequences keep every bone.
#
# MEASURED: 83 bones, 6 sequences - idle1, fidgetfit, fidgetnip, down, up,
# throw - and no reload and no fire among them. Posed in its own root's
# frame, `throw` moves the snark not at all: 0.00 units on all 36 of its
# bones across all 14 frames. The throw is an arm animation, and in VR the
# arm is the player's.
#
# The bite in the hand is damage over time in the game DLL, not geometry: it
# is neither a joint the player works nor a swept strike the player aims, so
# no `strike` line. The weak pistol-whip default applies, as it does to the
# penguin.

weapon v_squeak
match  bones 83  seqs 6  hash 0x9edc84de

type   thrown
fuse   0           # it decides

# ONE CARD, TWO GAMEDIRS - but NOT because the files match. valve_hd and
# gearbox_hd both fingerprint 83/6/0x9edc84de and their animation is
# identical to the hundredth of a unit, so this card binds both. The files
# are not byte-identical, though (139,180 vs 177,184 bytes): the difference
# is entirely in bones 0-46, the player's hands, which is why vertex extent
# reads 15.62 in valve_hd and 12.86 in gearbox_hd. Bones 47-82 - the snark
# itself - carry the same vertex counts in both. Nothing above is derived
# from a vertex, so the mesh difference does not reach this card.
#
# The SD models are a different weapon as far as binding goes and want their
# own cards: valve is 75 bones / 0x557c4f0f, gearbox is 74 / 0x6c318d57.
